---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Attribution from '../components/site/Attribution.astro';
import { MANUAL } from '../data/citation';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
---
<BaseLayout title="About" section="about" description="What C++ Atlas is, who it is for, and how it is built.">
  <div class="wrap-narrow about">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>A course in scientific modelling through C++</h1>
    </header>
    <p>
      The course does not try to cover every corner of the language. Its goal is to make a scientist able to read a mathematical model in a paper,
      understand its assumptions, design an algorithm for it, implement that algorithm in C++, verify that the code is correct, run
      simulation experiments, analyse the output and draw conclusions that say no more than the model supports.
    </p>
    <h2>How it is organised</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={url('/atlas/')}>atlas</a> is a map of the curriculum. The foundations of the language lie to the west, and the four
      modelling destinations lie to the east: algorithm design, numerical integration, stochastic modelling and individual-based
      modelling. A final region, scientific inference, is original to this site and connects the destinations to experiments,
      analysis and interpretation.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can travel in both directions: from a construct you have just learned to the models that use it, or from a model you want to
      build back to everything it requires.
    </p>
    <h2>Curriculum source</h2>
    <p>
      The curriculum follows <cite>{MANUAL.title}</cite> by {MANUAL.authors.join(', ')} ({MANUAL.institution}). Full details are on
      the <a href={url('/sources/')}>sources page</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="key"><Attribution label="Source" /></div>
    <h2>Design</h2>
    <p>
      Scientific figures and the colours of the code follow the <a href="https://akaminas.github.io/vellum/">Vellum</a> design system.
      The text is set in Calibri (or Carlito, its open twin, where Calibri is not installed) and the code in Source Code Pro. The pages
      come in a light and a dark theme: the button in the header switches between them, and the first visit follows your system setting.
    </p>
    <h2>Your data</h2>
    <p>
      There are no accounts and no tracking. Your level, goals, bookmarks and progress are stored only in your browser; the
      <a href={url('/progress/')}>progress page</a> exports, imports and deletes them.
    </p>
    <section class="maker" aria-labelledby="maker-title">
      <p class="maker-label">The maker</p>
      <h2 id="maker-title">Designed, drawn and coded by Alexandros Kaminas</h2>
      <p>PhD researcher at TRÊS, University of Groningen, modelling complex systems.</p>
      <p><a class="btn-outline" href="https://akaminas.github.io/">Meet the creator</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .about h2 { margin-top: 1.8em; padding-bottom: 6px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .maker { margin-top: 3.2em; padding: 24px 28px 8px; background: var(--panel); border-left: 4px solid var(--rule-heavy); border-radius: 0 3px 3px 0; }
  .maker h2 { margin: 0 0 10px; padding: 0; border: 0; font-size: 1.5rem; }
  .maker .maker-label { margin: 0 0 4px; font-size: var(--t-small); font-weight: 700; color: var(--v-secondary); }
  .maker p { text-align: left; }
</style>
